(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has a translucent skin-coloured papule over the distal interphalangeal joint of her index finger. The nail beyond it has a longitudinal groove. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Digital mucous cyst","Epidermoid cyst","Chronic paronychia","Acrochordon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — pressure on the nail matrix causes the groove. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm brown nodule on her lower leg. She thinks it appeared after an insect bite two years ago. Squeezing it laterally makes it retract beneath the surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatofibroma","Cutaneous melanoma","Keloid","Epidermoid cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the dimple sign, on the commonest site, after a bite. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who drives a lorry presents with sudden pain and swelling over his sacrum. There is a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant swelling in the gluteal cleft with a hair protruding from a small opening. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stage 2 pressure injury","Epidermoid cyst","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Pilonidal abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the natal cleft, the sinus opening and the protruding hair. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has several soft, skin-coloured, pedunculated papules on a thin stalk in both axillae and on her neck. They are asymptomatic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Acrochordon","Cutaneous neurofibroma","Seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — friction sites, obesity, female, and a narrow stalk with a broad tip. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 19-year-old has a rough lesion on the back of his hand with a cauliflower-like surface and small black dots at the centre. The skin lines stop at its edge. Squeezing it from the sides hurts more than pressing on it. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Clavus","Callus","Cutaneous horn","Verruca vulgaris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — interrupted skin lines, blackened centre, and pain on side pressure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man had an appendicectomy five weeks ago. The scar is raised, firm and red but stops exactly at the edges of the original incision. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatofibroma","Hypertrophic scar","Keloid","Foreign-body granuloma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it developed within four weeks and is confined to the wound margins. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a flat, blanchable, pink-purple patch on one cheek that has been present since birth and stops sharply at the midline. His parents ask what can be done about it. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["No treatment is required; tinted waterproof makeup, or pulsed dye laser","Oral propranolol as first line, with corticosteroids as an alternative","Surgical excision in infancy before it thickens","Observation only, because it fades during the first year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the laser selectively destroys the superficial target vessels. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with moderate acne has a family history of keloids. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Wait until the acne resolves on its own before starting any treatment","Begin silicone sheeting over the acne-prone areas now","Avoid all topical acne therapy, since it provokes fibrous overgrowth","Treat the acne early and appropriately"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acne is one of the named keloid triggers, and early appropriate treatment greatly increases the chance of scar-free healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient asks why her surgical scar has become stronger over the past year. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["Progressive replacement of collagen by adipose tissue","Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibers as the scar matures","Progressive proliferation of endothelial cells within the scar","Progressive accumulation of keratin beneath the scar surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the remodeling phase of wound healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A patient is offered compression therapy for a keloid. Which regimen matches the lecture?","choices":["Fifteen millimetres of mercury, continuously, for one month","Twenty-five millimetres of mercury, round the clock, six to twelve months","Twenty-five millimetres of mercury, twelve hours a day, for six weeks","Forty millimetres of mercury, overnight, for three months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the mechanism is not understood, but it possibly induces tissue hypoxia with fibroblast degeneration and subsequent collagen degradation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a firm, hard, conical projection on the helix of his ear that looks like a small animal horn. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Cryotherapy to the projection, with review in three months","Dermoscopy alone, which will identify the underlying lesion","Deep shave biopsy, to sample the tissue at the base","Reassure him, since a cutaneous horn is a benign keratin projection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is often no clinical feature separating benign from malignant. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Six weeks later the cyst is quiet and the patient wants it gone permanently. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Repeated intralesional steroid until it disappears","Cryotherapy to the overlying skin","Surgical removal of the entire capsule","Aspiration of the contents with a wide-bore needle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a small cyst of one to three centimetres can instead be punched and emptied. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a large keratoacanthoma on the nasal tip. Which approach does the lecture indicate?","choices":["Standard elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins regardless of site","Cryotherapy, which spares the nasal cartilage","Intralesional methotrexate as definitive treatment","Mohs surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is indicated for large or recurrent lesions, and for lesions in anatomic areas with cosmetic or functional considerations. This lesion is both. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient has a blind-ending track opening onto the skin over the coccyx. Which term describes it, and how does it differ from the alternative?","choices":["A fistula; a sinus instead is lined with keratin","A sinus; a fistula instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces","A fistula; a sinus instead connects two epithelium-lined surfaces","A sinus; a fistula instead is always congenital in origin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both usually arise from a preceding abscess. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm brown nodule on her lower leg that retracts beneath the skin when compressed from the sides. She is not bothered by it but wants to know her options. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Wide local excision is required in every case because of melanoma risk","Intralesional corticosteroid will flatten it over several months","Cryotherapy is the treatment of choice for pigmented dermal nodules","No treatment is needed; a shave or punch biopsy would remove it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in a small lesion that biopsy is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Larger lesions may need surgical excision instead. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has soft, yellow plaques on both medial upper eyelids. Beyond addressing the plaques themselves, what should be done for her?","choices":["Screen for hyperlipidemia","Screen for diabetes mellitus and check a haemoglobin A1c","Check thyroid function tests","Check liver function tests and a coagulation screen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — xanthelasma is associated with lipid disorders, and may signify an increased risk of cardiac disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 6-week-old former preterm girl has a bright red raised plaque on her scalp. Her mother says the area first looked pale, then developed fine red lines, and now asks whether it will need surgery. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Most involute on their own over several years","It will grow with her throughout life and will not involute","It will fade within her first year without any treatment","It will regress over three to six months after a period of stability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — complete involution in fifty per cent by age five, seventy per cent by seven and ninety per cent by nine, so serial observation is often all that is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus has a painful corn on his fifth toe. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Pare the lesion down in clinic and advise wider footwear","Excise the lesion with a five-millimetre margin","Refer him to podiatry","Start an over-the-counter salicylic acid product and review in a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags the diabetic patient specifically for podiatry referral. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which diagnostic testing does this patient's pilonidal disease require?","choices":["None; the diagnosis is clinical","Magnetic resonance imaging to map any sinus tracts","Punch biopsy of the sinus opening","Wound culture before any intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — no testing is usually needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man who uses a wheelchair has an ischial wound whose base is covered entirely by thick black eschar. You cannot see how deep it goes. Which stage is this?","choices":["Stage 4","Deep tissue pressure injury","Stage 3","Unstageable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — full-thickness loss whose extent is obscured by slough or eschar. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"You are counting café au lait macules on that same 9-year-old. How many are described as diagnostic, and what caveat applies?","choices":["Six or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis","Three or more; but only if they appear after puberty","Six or more; but the macules alone do not establish the diagnosis","Two or more; and the macules alone establish the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — over five millimetres prepubertal, over fifteen postpubertal. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A patient elects to have a benign lesion removed purely for appearance. What must she be told first?","choices":["That the lesion would become malignant if left in place","That lifelong surveillance of the site will be needed","The risk of pigmentary changes and the chance of recurrence","That histology is required on every excised specimen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both must be fully explained beforehand. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on both lower eyelids and upper cheeks. She asks about having them removed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The lesions will resolve on their own within a year","Removal is curative and recurrence does not occur","Treatment is for cosmesis only, and every option has a trade-off","The lesions must be removed because they can become malignant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — drugs such as oral isotretinoin carry an increased risk of recurrence, and removal procedures carry a risk of poor cosmetic results. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on his trunk that blanch with pressure and have accumulated over recent years. He asks whether removing them will stop new ones appearing. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Each one should be biopsied because they are premalignant","New lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them","Removing them prevents further lesions from forming","They will fade on their own within a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary unless they bother him. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a firm, movable, round nodule on his upper back with a small central pore that expresses cream-coloured pasty material. He asks whether it is a sebaceous cyst full of oil. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["It is a collection of lipid-laden macrophages","It is mucin extruded from a nearby joint","The contents are keratin, not sebum","Yes, it is a blocked sebaceous gland filled with sebum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is often called a sebaceous cyst because the material looks like sebum, but the deck states outright that it is not one. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 26-year-old with a keloid on the sternum wants it removed. Which is the most appropriate advice about surgical excision alone?","choices":["Recurrence is fifty to one hundred per cent, so steroid injection follows","Recurrence is under five per cent, so excision alone is usually curative","Excision is contraindicated in keloids because it always causes bleeding","Excision is preferred over all other options because it removes the fibrous tissue completely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the recurrent lesion is often larger than the original, which is why excision is never done alone. Combination therapy has the best success rates. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has several small vascular lesions with a central punctum and radiating vessels across his upper chest and arms. Which history is most important to take?","choices":["Birth history, feeding and family history of birthmarks","Alcohol use, liver-damaging medications, and hormone use","Footwear, occupation and use of hand tools","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck also says to ask about pregnancies. Spider angiomas are associated with estrogen excess and with cirrhosis and liver failure. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A patient's epidermoid cyst is red, hot and tender. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Postpone excision and settle the inflammation first","Excise it today, including the entire capsule","Incise and drain it, and take no further action","Biopsy it to exclude basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reduce it with an intralesional injection of triamcinolone, add antibiotics if needed, and operate a few weeks later when the cyst is quiet. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with very fair skin has a dome-shaped lesion with a central keratin-filled crater on his forearm that appeared six weeks ago and grew fast. He asks whether you can simply watch it, since he has read they go away. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Observe for three to six months, since regression is the natural history","Take a dermoscopy image and review in three months","Apply a topical keratolytic to flatten the crater","Excise or destroy it, because of possible malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — strong arguments support classifying keratoacanthoma as a variant of invasive squamous cell carcinoma, so the standard of care is not observation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient with a pyogenic granuloma opts against treatment. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["It will resolve within two weeks without any follow-up","It is benign and often resolves over months to years","It is premalignant and must be excised within a month","It is infectious and household contacts should be checked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — effective treatments exist if she changes her mind. If it recurs, early follow-up is appropriate, because small lesions are easier to treat than large ones. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 96"}]};
